{
  "result": "Every morning, the bakery on the corner opens before the street has decided what kind of day it will be. Its windows glow amber against the dark, and the smell of bread slips through the door whenever someone enters. By sunrise, a small queue has usually formed outside the shop.\n\nThe people waiting there rarely speak much at first. A nurse checks her phone after a long shift. A teenager balances a violin case against one knee. A man in a paint-stained jacket studies the clouds as though they might offer advice about the work ahead of him later today.\n\nInside, the baker moves with the quiet economy of someone who has repeated a task enough times to find freedom in it. She folds paper around warm loaves, remembers familiar orders, and sometimes adds an extra biscuit to a bag when she thinks a customer needs a little kindness today.\n\nNo one calls this remarkable. There are no speeches, no photographs, no announcement that a neighborhood is being held together. Yet the small exchanges accumulate: a door held open, a coin lent, a name remembered. They make the morning feel less like a collection of strangers passing through the streets.\n\nBy noon, the queue is gone and the windows reflect ordinary traffic. Crumbs remain on the counter. The baker washes her hands and begins preparing tomorrow\u2019s dough. Outside, the city continues its noisy business, carrying within it a few people who have been fed, noticed, and sent on their way.",
